    this.name = \"NotImplementedError\";\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:22:/** Fail-closed structured result for service/adapter/handler stubs. Uses LHC's\npackages/pi-lhc/src/shared/not-implemented.ts:30:      reason: `${operation} is not implemented yet (Epic 1 foundation stub)`,\npackages/pi-lhc/test/verify/replay.test.ts:6:// mocked intake, and never by writing the fixture's expected events directly\npackages/pi-lhc/test/verify/replay.test.ts:57:  if (!read.ok) throw new Error(`listEvents failed: ${read.error.reason}`);\npackages/pi-lhc/test/verify/replay.test.ts:112:    // The keys are the converter's real deterministic keys, not placeholders.\npackages/pi-lhc/test/verify/replay.test.ts:132:// ── TC-6.3: inspect overview/health reflect the captured session ─────────────\npackages/pi-lhc/test/verify/replay.test.ts:133:describe(\"Story 3: inspect overview/health reflect the captured session (TC-6.3)\", () => {\npackages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/picker.ts:26: *  filter over an unscoped list (anti-shim). With no threads for the cwd the\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/converter.ts:9://     record a durable, queryable gap (a `runtime_note`) so nothing is silently\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/converter.ts:13://     signal. Either way the session continues.\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/converter.ts:44:/** A durable, queryable gap marker for a rejected batch — a `runtime_note` so it\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/converter.ts:51:    eventKind: \"runtime_note\",\npackages/pi-lhc/src/capture/converter.ts:62:    eventKind: \"runtime_note\",\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:6:Build the walking skeleton for the pi-lhc extension: package scaffold, PI hook registration rail, plain-data-only state holder, stubbed LHC init/dispose seam, corpus fixture loader, and typed fail-closed stubs.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:13:**Objective:** Establish the loadable extension structure every later story builds on, with no stale PI context retained across hook boundaries and no stub that can fake successful capture.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:21:- Typed fail-closed stubs for unimplemented capture, inference, validation, and fork behavior.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:57:- This is foundation work with no AC owner, but later stories can be reward-hacked if stubs fake success.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:71:| Capture/inference/fork stubs | `packages/pi-lhc/src/capture/*`, `packages/pi-lhc/src/inference/*`, `packages/pi-lhc/src/lifecycle/fork.ts` |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:87:| None | `test/fixtures/corpus.test.ts`, extension-load smoke, package script checks | Foundation has no story-owned TC; smoke checks prove load, hook rail, fail-closed stubs, fixture validity, and verification-script availability. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:99:- Keep `state.ts` complete rather than stubbed: it is the shape that prevents stale PI context retention later.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/00-foundation.md:103:- No stub may report successful capture, derivation, validation, fork seeding, or replay before the owning story implements it.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:41:**AC-2.2:** LHC turn boundaries are derived from PI traffic, not from PI's per-agent-step `turn_end`. One LHC turn spans a user prompt and all subsequent agent activity until the next user prompt or the end of the agent run. The converter emits exactly one LHC `turn_end` event per LHC turn, at the agent run's completion — never one per PI `turn_end`, and never keyed off PI's per-agent-run `turnIndex` as a session counter.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:59:**AC-2.6:** Capture is duplicate-safe. Re-delivered events (reload, crash-replay) are recognized by idempotency key and skipped rather than recorded twice. On normal resume PI re-delivers no historical events, so duplication only arises on the reload and replay paths.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:61:**TC-2.7** — A re-delivered event (reload/replay) is skipped by idempotency key; a normal resume re-delivers no historical events.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:63:**AC-2.7:** A capture failure does not break the PI session, and does not vanish silently. When the thread is writable, a malformed or unmappable event records a durable, queryable gap that surfaces in thread health. When the thread store itself is unavailable, the failure surfaces as an extension diagnostic / health signal rather than a durable gap (the record cannot be written), and no exception propagates into the PI hook. Either way the session continues, and capture adds no perceptible latency to interactive use.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:65:**TC-2.8** — A malformed/unmappable event on a writable thread records a durable, queryable gap surfaced in health; an unavailable thread store surfaces an extension diagnostic / health signal with no durable gap. Both continue the session with no exception reaching the hook.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:67:**AC-2.8:** Runtime changes that PI fires only in-stream — model selection (`model_select`) and thinking-level selection (`thinking_level_select`) — are captured in order as `runtime_note` events carrying the change (the new model or level, and the previous one). These are recorded at the moment they fire because no durable record holds them otherwise; nothing in this epic consumes them, and their presence in the thread is what lets later epics attribute a turn to the model that produced it.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:69:**TC-2.9** — A `model_select` and a `thinking_level_select` each record an ordered `runtime_note` event carrying the new and previous values; ordering relative to surrounding messages is preserved.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:126:| TC-2.7 | `test/capture/parallel-and-errors.test.ts` | Re-delivered event is skipped by idempotency key; normal resume does not replay historical messages. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:128:| TC-2.9 | `test/capture/converter.test.ts` | Model and thinking-level changes become ordered `runtime_note` events with new and previous values. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:134:| Idempotency / Retry | `test/capture/idempotency.test.ts` | Replaying the same corpus twice produces no duplicate events; re-delivered keys return `skipped`. | Single-event dedup does not prove reload/replay safety across a corpus. |\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:148:- Do not add new intake event kinds for runtime model/thinking changes in this story; use `runtime_note`.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:154:- Evidence: capture tests assert stored events, duplicate skips, no hook exception, and exact turn boundaries through the real LHC intake path.\ndocs/specs/02-pi-lhc/01-connector-core/stories/02-event-capture-and-turn-derivation.md:173:- Capture failures are visible and do not break the PI session.\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts:15:import { fakeModelCallFailure, fakeModelCallRouter, fakeModelCallText } from \"../fixtures/model-call.js\";\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inference/startup-validation.test.ts:39:    sessionManager: { getEntries: () => [] },\npackages/pi-lhc/test/inf